{"duration_api_ms":196844,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"6ef62033-0172-4643-99eb-66bd9480d174","total_cost_usd":0.5720225999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":14,"cache_creation_input_tokens":68591,"cache_read_input_tokens":269137,"output_tokens":27017,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":13918},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":68591,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":14563,"cache_read_input_tokens":74812,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2235,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2235},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-5-5":{"inputTokens":5339,"outputTokens":26,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.0005469,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5-5":{"inputTokens":14,"outputTokens":27017,"cacheReadInputTokens":269137,"cacheCreationInputTokens":68591,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.5714756999999999,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":13918,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":68591,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":12,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer\":\"model_equation_auditor\",\"paper_id\":\"bicycle-infrastructure-paper\",\"run_status\":\"ok\",\"summary\":\"The paper has no structural model and no estimated regression tables. Its only formal content is two proposed specifications (an ordered model of date precision, eq. 1, and a difference-in-differences model of listing mentions, eq. 2, both in Section 6.3), the 2x2 agreement tables, and descriptive count tables. I audited those equations, their prose interpretation, and the notation, counts and definitions in the tables and appendix. The main problems are: the Table 9 classification rule contradicts its own worked example; footnote 3 contradicts Table 3 and Section 4.1 about how the Stord article was coded; the '2 of 34 articles' statistic mixes projects with articles; the Attention variable is undefined or multi-valued across eqs. (1) and (2); eq. (2) has index and lower-order-term gaps; and the Section 6.3 remedy for misdating does not follow from the setup. Parser-quality guidance was respected: page text was used instead of the structured table files, and equation fragments were checked against the page text and the page images.\",\"findings\":[{\"id\":\"MODEL-001\",\"category\":\"assumption_mismatch\",\"finding_summary\":\"The Table 9 classification rule ('first type in the table') contradicts its own worked example, because 'bysykkelstativ' contains 'stativ', a Parking & storage part listed before Infrastructure.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"location\":{\"page\":18,\"page_label\":\"18\",\"section\":\"A.3.2 Classification of cycling mentions\",\"text_quote\":\"given the first type in the table.\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"If a word fits more than one type, it is given the first type in the table; words containing 'bysykkel', such as 'bysykkelstativ', are counted as infrastructure.\",\"assessment\":\"no\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"Table 9 lists types in the order: The word 'bicycle', Proximity, Parking & storage, Leisure, Infrastructure, Other. Parking & storage includes the part 'stativ' (rack), and Infrastructure includes 'bysykkel'. 'bysykkelstativ' matches both. Under the stated first-match rule it would be Parking & storage. The text instead says it is counted as infrastructure, and Figure 2 shows the bysykkelstativ bar (0.5%) in the Infrastructure colour (blue). The type shares in Table 8 therefore rest on a precedence rule that the appendix describes inconsistently. The text does not show which rule was actually implemented.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"Appendix A.3.2 page text\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_018.md\",\"page\":18,\"page_label\":\"18\",\"section\":\"A.3.2\",\"text_quote\":\"given the first type in the table.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-03dcf11e0f4853cb90843dbe\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Rule and example are in the A.3.2 prose; the type order is from Table 9 in the same page text.\"},{\"id\":\"S2\",\"type\":\"figure\",\"label\":\"Figure 2 page 16 image\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/page_images/page_016.png\",\"page\":16,\"page_label\":\"16\",\"section\":\"A.2 Figure 2\",\"text_quote\":null,\"url\":null,\"object_id\":null,\"correspondence\":\"visual\",\"verification_note\":\"Inspected the Figure 2 bar for 'bysykkelstativ (city bike station) 0.5%': blue, matching the Infrastructure legend entry. No registry object id was looked up for this image.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"First-type rule applied to bysykkelstativ gives infrastructure\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\",\"S2\"],\"relation\":\"contradicts\",\"note\":\"'stativ' is in the earlier Parking & storage row, so the first-type rule gives Parking & storage.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"State the actual precedence rule (for example, specific compounds such as 'bysykkel' override, or reorder the table) and make the Table 9 order, the worked example, and the Table 8 and Figure 2 categories consistent.\"},{\"id\":\"MODEL-002\",\"category\":\"notation_inconsistency\",\"finding_summary\":\"Footnote 3 says the Stord project was coded as not relevant by both human coders, but Table 3 and Section 4.1 show the second coder coded the Stord article as relevant.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"location\":{\"page\":10,\"page_label\":\"10\",\"section\":\"6 Further Uses, footnote 3\",\"text_quote\":\"was coded as not\",\"precision\":\"partial\"},\"claim_text\":\"The 44th project, in Stord, appears only in the pilot and was coded as not relevant by both human coders (Section 4).\",\"assessment\":\"no\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"Table 3 has one article coded Not relevant by the pilot coder and Relevant by the second coder, and Section 4.1 says the single disagreement article is the Stord dispute. So the second coder did not code it as not relevant. Section 4.2 says the LLM also coded it relevant, which fits the LLM's four relevant articles (3+1) and the footnote's 'four from the pilot run'. Footnote 3 is thus inconsistent with the validation section, and it is unclear on what basis a project with no reported opening, only a passing mention of a path completed the year before, is counted among the 44 projects. The 96 = 92 + 4 and 44 = 43 + 1 reconciliations also cannot be checked against retained pilot-to-rounds overlap information.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 10 text (footnote 3)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_010.md\",\"page\":10,\"page_label\":\"10\",\"section\":\"footnote 3\",\"text_quote\":\"coded as not\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-e9b90e0f75ce05ff1ce150cc\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"The footnote spans lines; the short quote is the contiguous fragment. The full wording ('relevant by both human coders (Section 4)') is in the same footnote. Footnote marker 3 is attached to the word 'projects3' in the body text.\"},{\"id\":\"S2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 7 text (Table 3 and Section 4.1)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_007.md\",\"page\":7,\"page_label\":\"7\",\"section\":\"4.1\",\"text_quote\":\"The only article they disagree on covers a\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-4e6da301f188ae5c7e5d0a63\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Table 3 body cells (3, 0, 3; 1, 30, 31; 4, 30, 34) read from the page text, not from the structured table file.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Stord article coded not relevant by both human coders\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\",\"S2\"],\"relation\":\"contradicts\",\"note\":\"Table 3 shows the second coder coded the one disagreement article as relevant.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"Correct footnote 3 to say which coders and the LLM coded the Stord article relevant, state why a project without a reported opening is included in the 44, and document the 96-article and 44-project reconciliation with the pilot articles and rounds 2-4.\"},{\"id\":\"MODEL-003\",\"category\":\"sign_or_definition_error\",\"finding_summary\":\"The statistic 'Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place' divides a count of distinct openings (projects) by a count of articles.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"location\":{\"page\":4,\"page_label\":\"4\",\"section\":\"3.1.2 Pilot sample\",\"text_quote\":\"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place.\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place.\",\"assessment\":\"no\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"Figure 1 labels the 2 as 'Distinct openings that took place', and its caption says 2 of 70 search hits concern an opening (Homborsund and Stod/Steinkjer). Section 3.1.4 says five articles were relevant: four about Homborsund and one about Stod, with two Homborsund articles published before the opening. So the number of articles concerning an opening that took place is 3 to 5 depending on how pre-opening articles are treated, not 2. The numerator is projects and the denominator is articles. The same two-unit mix recurs in the Figure 1 caption ('2 of 70 search hits') and the abstract ('only two actual openings'). The arithmetic 2/34 = 5.9% is correct but mixes units.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 4 text (Section 3.1.2)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_004.md\",\"page\":4,\"page_label\":\"4\",\"section\":\"3.1.2\",\"text_quote\":\"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-1a119a3cb70f2153fc56dd97\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Figure 1 labels are in the same page text.\"},{\"id\":\"S2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 5 text (Section 3.1.4)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_005.md\",\"page\":5,\"page_label\":\"5\",\"section\":\"3.1.4\",\"text_quote\":\"Of the five relevant articles, only two unique openings were found.\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-9d16c139938370d7a97e77c6\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Sentence is on page 5 in the page text.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"2 of 34 articles concern an opening\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\",\"S2\"],\"relation\":\"contradicts\",\"note\":\"2 is the number of distinct openings; five articles were coded relevant to those two openings.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"Restate the yield in one unit, for example '5 of 34 articles (3 after excluding pre-opening articles) concern the 2 distinct openings', and align the Figure 1 caption and abstract.\"},{\"id\":\"MODEL-004\",\"category\":\"notation_inconsistency\",\"finding_summary\":\"Attention_p is defined in eq. (1) as one of three different measures (a ceremony indicator, a count of stories, a count of outlets) with a single coefficient, and it is not defined for eq. (2), where δ is interpreted as the effect of the opening being 'well known locally'.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3 Proposed econometric analysis\",\"text_quote\":\"is measured by a reported ceremony, the number of distinct stories or the\",\"precision\":\"partial\"},\"claim_text\":\"Attention_p is measured by a reported ceremony, the number of distinct stories or the number of outlets; δ > 0 means more mentions where the opening is well known locally.\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"Eq. (1) uses one variable and one scalar β, but the text lists a binary indicator and two counts, whose coefficients have different units. Eq. (2) uses Attention_p in the triple interaction without repeating or choosing a definition. The hypothesis for δ interprets it as local salience, whereas a ceremony report or a count of outlets is a coverage measure. The stories and outlets counts are also mechanically related to date precision (more articles give more chances that one states the date), so the one variable cannot be read as the same construct in both models. The paper calls these specifications examples, so this is a notation and definition gap rather than an estimation error.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 11 text (eqs. 1 and 2 and prose)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md\",\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3\",\"text_quote\":\"is measured by a reported ceremony, the number of distinct stories or the\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Equation glyphs and subscripts are fragmented in extraction, so the prose definition is quoted rather than the equation; the equation layout was read from the page text.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Attention_p enters (1) and (2) as one variable\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"Three candidate measures with different scales are given for one coefficient; no definition is given for (2).\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"Choose one operational definition of Attention_p (or index the variants, for example Ceremony_p, Stories_p, Outlets_p) and state the same definition in (2); say how δ differs between binary and count measures.\"},{\"id\":\"MODEL-005\",\"category\":\"notation_inconsistency\",\"finding_summary\":\"In eq. (2), Near_j is indexed only by listing j although it is defined relative to project p, and the area index a(j) and the comparison group are not defined.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"low\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3 Effect on housing listings\",\"text_quote\":\"Nearj indicates the inner distance band\",\"precision\":\"exact\"},\"claim_text\":\"Y_jpt indicates that listing j near project p in month t mentions cycling; Near_j indicates the inner distance band; α_a(j) and λ_pt are area and project-by-month fixed effects.\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"Y has indices j, p and t, so the data appear to be listing-by-project pairs, but Near_j carries no p index. A listing within the inner band of one project may lie in the outer band of another. The text says only 'the inner distance band', so the band widths and the 'further away' comparison group are not defined. The area a(j) is also not defined and may be at a different level from the distance bands. The paper presents the model as a possible example, so this is an incomplete notation issue, not a computational error.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 11 text (eq. 2 and definitions)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md\",\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3\",\"text_quote\":\"Nearj indicates the inner distance band\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Equation (2) read from the page text; subscripts are fragmented in extraction.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Near_j is a listing-level indicator in a listing-by-project panel\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"Near depends on the project but is not indexed by p.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"Index the indicator as Near_jp, define the distance bands and the comparison group, and define the area a(j).\"},{\"id\":\"MODEL-006\",\"category\":\"equation_text_mismatch\",\"finding_summary\":\"Eq. (2) interacts Near×Post with Attention_p but includes no Near_j×Attention_p term, and the stated fixed effects do not obviously absorb it, so δ is not a standard triple-difference coefficient.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"confidence\":\"low\",\"location\":{\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3 Effect on housing listings\",\"text_quote\":\"are area and project-by-month fixed\",\"precision\":\"partial\"},\"claim_text\":\"Eq. (2) tests whether openings increase mentions nearby (θ > 0) and more so when the opening is well known locally (δ > 0).\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"The displayed regressors are Near×Post and Near×Post×Attention, with area fixed effects α_a(j) and project-by-month fixed effects λ_pt. Post and Post×Attention are absorbed by λ_pt, because both vary only by project and month. Near_j×Attention_p varies across listings within a project, so it is absorbed by neither fixed effect set unless area effects are interacted with project, and the paper does not say so. Without that term, δ also picks up any baseline difference between near and far listings that scales with Attention_p. This is a specification gap in a proposed model. The paper does not state the estimand, so low confidence.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 11 text (eq. 2)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md\",\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3\",\"text_quote\":\"are area and project-by-month fixed\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Fragment from the sentence defining the fixed effects; the equation itself is fragmented in extraction.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"δ identifies the extra effect of local attention\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"The lower-order Near×Attention term is missing and not clearly absorbed.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"Include Near×Attention_p (or fixed effects that absorb it) and state which terms are absorbed by α_a(j) and λ_pt.\"},{\"id\":\"MODEL-007\",\"category\":\"model_interpretation_overreach\",\"finding_summary\":\"The hypothesis θ > 0 is described as 'openings increase mentions of cycling nearby', but in eq. (2) θ is the Near×Post effect only at Attention_p = 0.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"low\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3 Effect on housing listings\",\"text_quote\":\"openings increase mentions of cycling nearby\",\"precision\":\"partial\"},\"claim_text\":\"The hypotheses are θ > 0 and δ > 0: openings increase mentions of cycling nearby, and more so when the opening is well known locally.\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"With the interaction δ Near×Post×Attention in the model, θ is the effect for Attention_p = 0, which is a project without a ceremony if Attention is binary, or zero stories or outlets if it is a count (the latter cannot occur for projects identified from articles). The average effect of openings across projects is θ + δ·E[Attention]. The prose attributes the average effect to θ alone.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 11 text (hypotheses)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md\",\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3\",\"text_quote\":\"openings increase mentions of cycling nearby\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Quote is part of the hypothesis sentence.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"θ > 0 means openings increase nearby mentions\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"θ is conditional on Attention_p = 0 given the interaction term.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"Define θ as the effect at Attention_p = 0 or centre Attention_p, and state the average-effect hypothesis explicitly.\"},{\"id\":\"MODEL-008\",\"category\":\"model_interpretation_overreach\",\"finding_summary\":\"The Section 6.3 diagnosis and remedy for misdated openings ('bias toward zero'; restrict model (2) to precisely dated projects) do not follow from the displayed setup, because misdating differs by Attention and the precisely dated sample is almost all ceremony projects.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"medium\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":12,\"page_label\":\"12\",\"section\":\"6.3 Proposed econometric analysis\",\"text_quote\":\"bias the results toward zero\",\"precision\":\"partial\"},\"claim_text\":\"If projects with less attention are dated less precisely, this could bias the results toward zero; if so, model (2) should only use precisely dated projects.\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"Eq. (2) estimates θ and δ. If Post is misassigned more for low-Attention projects, the attenuation differs by Attention, so δ can be biased upward and not only toward zero. Separately, the paper reports that 17 of the 19 day-dated projects have a ceremony (2 of the 26 without a ceremony are day-dated). Restricting to day-precision projects therefore leaves almost no variation in a ceremony-based Attention_p, which is the variable δ relies on. The remedy and the Attention interaction cannot both be implemented as described.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 12 text (bias discussion)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_012.md\",\"page\":12,\"page_label\":\"12\",\"section\":\"6.3\",\"text_quote\":\"bias the results toward zero\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-a6c6d31e7be45f25b775aa47\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Fragment of the bias sentence.\"},{\"id\":\"S2\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 11 text (ceremony and precision counts)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md\",\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3\",\"text_quote\":\"Of these, 17 can be dated to the day\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"The full sentence continues 'compared with 2 of the 26 projects without a reported ceremony'.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Misdating biases results toward zero and restricting to precisely dated projects fixes it\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\",\"S2\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"Differential misclassification can bias δ upward, and the precise subsample has little variation in ceremony.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"State the direction of bias separately for θ and δ, and either use an Attention measure that varies within precisely dated projects or describe a different remedy, such as a date-uncertainty window.\"},{\"id\":\"MODEL-009\",\"category\":\"model_interpretation_overreach\",\"finding_summary\":\"The statement that date precision 'depends on how much attention an opening receives' is drawn from 17 of 18 ceremony projects versus 2 of 26 non-ceremony projects, but a reported opening ceremony is likely to state the opening date by construction.\",\"issue_type\":\"manuscript_issue\",\"severity\":\"low\",\"confidence\":\"medium\",\"location\":{\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3 Attention and date precision\",\"text_quote\":\"Of these, 17 can be dated to the day\",\"precision\":\"partial\"},\"claim_text\":\"This suggests that the precision of the dates is not random but depends on how much attention an opening receives.\",\"assessment\":\"partially\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":null,\"evidence_summary\":\"The comparison is descriptive and has no controls, as the paper acknowledges by proposing eq. (1). The ceremony indicator is built from the same articles that are used to date the project, so an article reporting a ceremony will usually state the opening day. The pattern therefore does not separate attention from a mechanical link between the Attention measure and the dating source. The paper uses 'suggests', so this is a qualification rather than a contradiction.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 11 text\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md\",\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3\",\"text_quote\":\"Of these, 17 can be dated to the day\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Counts quoted from the paragraph preceding eq. (1).\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Date precision depends on attention\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\"],\"relation\":\"partial\",\"note\":\"Raw 17/18 versus 2/26 contrast; ceremony articles may state the date mechanically.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"Add that ceremony reporting may mechanically include the date, and ensure the eq. (1) test uses an Attention measure independent of how the date was found.\"},{\"id\":\"MODEL-010\",\"category\":\"cannot_verify\",\"finding_summary\":\"Eq. (1) is described as an ordered model but gives no cut-points, no distribution for u_p and no ordering convention for the 'none, year, month, day' categories.\",\"issue_type\":\"cannot_verify\",\"severity\":\"low\",\"confidence\":\"low\",\"location\":{\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3 Attention and date precision\",\"text_quote\":\"ordered model of date precision\",\"precision\":\"partial\"},\"claim_text\":\"A possible extension is to test this at project level with an ordered model of date precision.\",\"assessment\":\"cannot_verify\",\"cannot_verify_reason\":\"The model is described only in one sentence and the equation glyphs are fragmented in extraction, so the link function, cut-points and category coding cannot be checked against a full specification.\",\"evidence_summary\":\"Eq. (1) shows a latent index Precision*_p = β Attention_p + x'_p γ + u_p. The text names four observed categories and states that a positive β implies higher precision, but does not give the mapping from the latent variable to the categories or the distribution of u_p (ordered probit or logit). Table 6 uses 'Not stated' for the lowest category, while the text says 'none', and the date-precision field in Table 5 is 'day precision'. These do not conflict but the coding is not tied together. The paper presents the specification as an example, so the missing detail is a disclosure limit.\",\"source_objects\":[{\"id\":\"S1\",\"type\":\"text\",\"label\":\"page 11 text (eq. 1 description)\",\"path\":\"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md\",\"page\":11,\"page_label\":\"11\",\"section\":\"6.3\",\"text_quote\":\"ordered model of date precision\",\"url\":null,\"object_id\":\"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df\",\"correspondence\":\"text\",\"verification_note\":\"Prose describing eq. (1); the equation itself is fragmented in extraction.\"}],\"claim_evidence_links\":[{\"claim_text\":\"Eq. (1) defines an ordered model of date precision\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"S1\"],\"relation\":\"cannot_verify\",\"note\":\"Link function and cut-points are not stated.\"}],\"numeric_check\":null,\"suggested_fix\":\"State the link function (probit or logit), the cut-points, the ordering of the four categories, and match the category names with Table 6.\"}],\"notes\":[\"The paper has no estimated regression tables, so the dependent-variable, fixed-effect and estimator cross-check applies only to the proposed eqs. (1) and (2), which are described as examples.\",\"Equation glyphs and subscripts in the extracted text are fragmented, so findings on eqs. (1) and (2) rely on the prose definitions and on the equation layout as read from the page text; exact variable typography was not checked at glyph level.\",\"Table 3 and Table 4 body cells and the Table 8 counts and shares were read from the page text, not from the structured table files, which the parser-quality auditor flagged as unreliable.\",\"Source registry object ids were copied from the page-level text spans in source_registry.json. The page 16 image used for Figure 2 had no registry id looked up, so its object_id is null with correspondence=visual.\"],\"completion\":{\"version\":1,\"state\":\"complete\",\"scope_description\":\"Audit of model, equation, notation and text-equation consistency for the full parsed paper: eqs. (1) and (2) in Section 6.3, their definitions and hypotheses, the agreement tables (Tables 3 and 4), the count and share tables (Tables 6, 7 and 8), the classification rule in Appendix A.3.2 and Table 9, and the associated text, footnotes and Figure 2.\",\"checks_completed\":[\"Read the full parsed text and the parser-quality output before auditing\",\"Cross-checked eq. (1) and eq. (2) against their variable definitions, fixed effects, hypotheses and prose interpretation\",\"Checked arithmetic and definitions: 2 of 34 articles, 5 of 34 relevant, Table 3 and 4 totals and 97% agreement, Table 6 precision sum (19+10+9+5=43), 17+2=19 day-dated projects, 18+26=44, Table 8 shares and totals (266 625 + 433 954 = 700 579)\",\"Compared Table 9 classification rule with the worked example and the Figure 2 bar colours\",\"Compared footnote 3 with Table 3 and Section 4.1\",\"Compared the Section 3.1.2 yield statistic with Figure 1 and Section 3.1.4\"],\"missing_checks\":[],\"evidence_limitations\":[\"Equation glyphs and subscripts are fragmented in the extracted text, so exact typography of eqs. (1) and (2) was not checked at glyph level.\",\"The paper contains no estimated regression tables or structural model, so no estimator, reference-group or fixed-effect checks of reported results were possible.\",\"The overlap between pilot articles and rounds 2-4 is not documented, so the 96-article and 44-project reconciliation could not be verified.\"]},\"coverage\":[{\"item_id\":\"assigned_remit\",\"source_object_ids\":[],\"claim\":null,\"check_performed\":\"Read the full parsed text and audited eqs. (1) and (2), their definitions and interpretation, the agreement tables, the count tables, the Table 9 classification rule and the related text and footnotes.\",\"status\":\"linked_finding\",\"reason\":\"Ten findings: Table 9 precedence rule versus example (MODEL-001), footnote 3 versus Table 3 (MODEL-002), the 2 of 34 unit mismatch (MODEL-003), Attention definition (MODEL-004), eq. (2) notation (MODEL-005), missing lower-order term (MODEL-006), θ interpretation (MODEL-007), misdating remedy (MODEL-008), ceremony-precision overreach (MODEL-009), and ordered-model detail (MODEL-010). Scope was exhaustive for equations and sampled for descriptive tables.\",\"finding_ids\":[\"MODEL-001\",\"MODEL-002\",\"MODEL-003\",\"MODEL-004\",\"MODEL-005\",\"MODEL-006\",\"MODEL-007\",\"MODEL-008\",\"MODEL-009\",\"MODEL-010\"],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:agreement-tables\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-4e6da301f188ae5c7e5d0a63\"],\"claim\":\"Human-human and human-LLM relevance agreement are both 97% (33 of 34 articles), as shown in Tables 3 and 4.\",\"check_performed\":\"Recomputed 3+30=33 and 33/34 from the page-7 text; read the Table 3 totals (3, 31, 4, 30, 34) and compared with the prose and with the Table 4 note on the majority of three runs.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"Counts and the 97% figure are consistent (33/34 = 97.1%). Table 4 body cells were taken from the page text, not the structured file.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"central\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:table8-shares\",\"source_object_ids\":[],\"claim\":\"Table 8 and the Appendix A.2 text report 266 625 listings (38.1%) mentioning cycling out of 700 579.\",\"check_performed\":\"Checked 266 625 + 433 954 = 700 579 and the shares 38.1, 13.4, 10.8, 10.7, 9.8, 3.8 and 1.0 against the counts, and the sum of type counts against the multi-type note.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"Arithmetic is consistent within rounding; the type rows exceed the total, as the table note says. Sampled arithmetic only, not a full recomputation of Figure 2.\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"peripheral\",\"scope\":\"sampled\"},{\"item_id\":\"claim:table6-counts\",\"source_object_ids\":[\"OBJ-e9b90e0f75ce05ff1ce150cc\"],\"claim\":\"Table 6 reports 43 projects (Day 19, Month 10, Year 9, Not stated 5) and 29 dated to the day or month.\",\"check_performed\":\"Summed the precision categories (19+10+9+5=43) and 19+10=29 from the page-10 text; compared 17+2=19 day-dated projects in Section 6.3 with the Day count.\",\"status\":\"checked_no_issue\",\"reason\":\"Counts agree; the 44-project base in Section 6.3 adds the Stord project (see MODEL-002).\",\"finding_ids\":[],\"attempted\":true,\"importance\":\"peripheral\",\"scope\":\"exhaustive\"}]}","structured_output":{"reviewer":"model_equation_auditor","paper_id":"bicycle-infrastructure-paper","run_status":"ok","summary":"The paper has no structural model and no estimated regression tables. Its only formal content is two proposed specifications (an ordered model of date precision, eq. 1, and a difference-in-differences model of listing mentions, eq. 2, both in Section 6.3), the 2x2 agreement tables, and descriptive count tables. I audited those equations, their prose interpretation, and the notation, counts and definitions in the tables and appendix. The main problems are: the Table 9 classification rule contradicts its own worked example; footnote 3 contradicts Table 3 and Section 4.1 about how the Stord article was coded; the '2 of 34 articles' statistic mixes projects with articles; the Attention variable is undefined or multi-valued across eqs. (1) and (2); eq. (2) has index and lower-order-term gaps; and the Section 6.3 remedy for misdating does not follow from the setup. Parser-quality guidance was respected: page text was used instead of the structured table files, and equation fragments were checked against the page text and the page images.","findings":[{"id":"MODEL-001","category":"assumption_mismatch","finding_summary":"The Table 9 classification rule ('first type in the table') contradicts its own worked example, because 'bysykkelstativ' contains 'stativ', a Parking & storage part listed before Infrastructure.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"medium","confidence":"high","location":{"page":18,"page_label":"18","section":"A.3.2 Classification of cycling mentions","text_quote":"given the first type in the table.","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"If a word fits more than one type, it is given the first type in the table; words containing 'bysykkel', such as 'bysykkelstativ', are counted as infrastructure.","assessment":"no","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"Table 9 lists types in the order: The word 'bicycle', Proximity, Parking & storage, Leisure, Infrastructure, Other. Parking & storage includes the part 'stativ' (rack), and Infrastructure includes 'bysykkel'. 'bysykkelstativ' matches both. Under the stated first-match rule it would be Parking & storage. The text instead says it is counted as infrastructure, and Figure 2 shows the bysykkelstativ bar (0.5%) in the Infrastructure colour (blue). The type shares in Table 8 therefore rest on a precedence rule that the appendix describes inconsistently. The text does not show which rule was actually implemented.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"Appendix A.3.2 page text","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_018.md","page":18,"page_label":"18","section":"A.3.2","text_quote":"given the first type in the table.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-03dcf11e0f4853cb90843dbe","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Rule and example are in the A.3.2 prose; the type order is from Table 9 in the same page text."},{"id":"S2","type":"figure","label":"Figure 2 page 16 image","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/page_images/page_016.png","page":16,"page_label":"16","section":"A.2 Figure 2","text_quote":null,"url":null,"object_id":null,"correspondence":"visual","verification_note":"Inspected the Figure 2 bar for 'bysykkelstativ (city bike station) 0.5%': blue, matching the Infrastructure legend entry. No registry object id was looked up for this image."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"First-type rule applied to bysykkelstativ gives infrastructure","source_object_ids":["S1","S2"],"relation":"contradicts","note":"'stativ' is in the earlier Parking & storage row, so the first-type rule gives Parking & storage."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"State the actual precedence rule (for example, specific compounds such as 'bysykkel' override, or reorder the table) and make the Table 9 order, the worked example, and the Table 8 and Figure 2 categories consistent."},{"id":"MODEL-002","category":"notation_inconsistency","finding_summary":"Footnote 3 says the Stord project was coded as not relevant by both human coders, but Table 3 and Section 4.1 show the second coder coded the Stord article as relevant.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"medium","confidence":"high","location":{"page":10,"page_label":"10","section":"6 Further Uses, footnote 3","text_quote":"was coded as not","precision":"partial"},"claim_text":"The 44th project, in Stord, appears only in the pilot and was coded as not relevant by both human coders (Section 4).","assessment":"no","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"Table 3 has one article coded Not relevant by the pilot coder and Relevant by the second coder, and Section 4.1 says the single disagreement article is the Stord dispute. So the second coder did not code it as not relevant. Section 4.2 says the LLM also coded it relevant, which fits the LLM's four relevant articles (3+1) and the footnote's 'four from the pilot run'. Footnote 3 is thus inconsistent with the validation section, and it is unclear on what basis a project with no reported opening, only a passing mention of a path completed the year before, is counted among the 44 projects. The 96 = 92 + 4 and 44 = 43 + 1 reconciliations also cannot be checked against retained pilot-to-rounds overlap information.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 10 text (footnote 3)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_010.md","page":10,"page_label":"10","section":"footnote 3","text_quote":"coded as not","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-e9b90e0f75ce05ff1ce150cc","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"The footnote spans lines; the short quote is the contiguous fragment. The full wording ('relevant by both human coders (Section 4)') is in the same footnote. Footnote marker 3 is attached to the word 'projects3' in the body text."},{"id":"S2","type":"text","label":"page 7 text (Table 3 and Section 4.1)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_007.md","page":7,"page_label":"7","section":"4.1","text_quote":"The only article they disagree on covers a","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-4e6da301f188ae5c7e5d0a63","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Table 3 body cells (3, 0, 3; 1, 30, 31; 4, 30, 34) read from the page text, not from the structured table file."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Stord article coded not relevant by both human coders","source_object_ids":["S1","S2"],"relation":"contradicts","note":"Table 3 shows the second coder coded the one disagreement article as relevant."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"Correct footnote 3 to say which coders and the LLM coded the Stord article relevant, state why a project without a reported opening is included in the 44, and document the 96-article and 44-project reconciliation with the pilot articles and rounds 2-4."},{"id":"MODEL-003","category":"sign_or_definition_error","finding_summary":"The statistic 'Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place' divides a count of distinct openings (projects) by a count of articles.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"medium","confidence":"high","location":{"page":4,"page_label":"4","section":"3.1.2 Pilot sample","text_quote":"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place.","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place.","assessment":"no","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"Figure 1 labels the 2 as 'Distinct openings that took place', and its caption says 2 of 70 search hits concern an opening (Homborsund and Stod/Steinkjer). Section 3.1.4 says five articles were relevant: four about Homborsund and one about Stod, with two Homborsund articles published before the opening. So the number of articles concerning an opening that took place is 3 to 5 depending on how pre-opening articles are treated, not 2. The numerator is projects and the denominator is articles. The same two-unit mix recurs in the Figure 1 caption ('2 of 70 search hits') and the abstract ('only two actual openings'). The arithmetic 2/34 = 5.9% is correct but mixes units.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 4 text (Section 3.1.2)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_004.md","page":4,"page_label":"4","section":"3.1.2","text_quote":"Only 2 of the 34 unique articles (about 6 percent) concern an opening that took place.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-1a119a3cb70f2153fc56dd97","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Figure 1 labels are in the same page text."},{"id":"S2","type":"text","label":"page 5 text (Section 3.1.4)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_005.md","page":5,"page_label":"5","section":"3.1.4","text_quote":"Of the five relevant articles, only two unique openings were found.","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-9d16c139938370d7a97e77c6","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Sentence is on page 5 in the page text."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"2 of 34 articles concern an opening","source_object_ids":["S1","S2"],"relation":"contradicts","note":"2 is the number of distinct openings; five articles were coded relevant to those two openings."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"Restate the yield in one unit, for example '5 of 34 articles (3 after excluding pre-opening articles) concern the 2 distinct openings', and align the Figure 1 caption and abstract."},{"id":"MODEL-004","category":"notation_inconsistency","finding_summary":"Attention_p is defined in eq. (1) as one of three different measures (a ceremony indicator, a count of stories, a count of outlets) with a single coefficient, and it is not defined for eq. (2), where δ is interpreted as the effect of the opening being 'well known locally'.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"medium","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3 Proposed econometric analysis","text_quote":"is measured by a reported ceremony, the number of distinct stories or the","precision":"partial"},"claim_text":"Attention_p is measured by a reported ceremony, the number of distinct stories or the number of outlets; δ > 0 means more mentions where the opening is well known locally.","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"Eq. (1) uses one variable and one scalar β, but the text lists a binary indicator and two counts, whose coefficients have different units. Eq. (2) uses Attention_p in the triple interaction without repeating or choosing a definition. The hypothesis for δ interprets it as local salience, whereas a ceremony report or a count of outlets is a coverage measure. The stories and outlets counts are also mechanically related to date precision (more articles give more chances that one states the date), so the one variable cannot be read as the same construct in both models. The paper calls these specifications examples, so this is a notation and definition gap rather than an estimation error.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 11 text (eqs. 1 and 2 and prose)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md","page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3","text_quote":"is measured by a reported ceremony, the number of distinct stories or the","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Equation glyphs and subscripts are fragmented in extraction, so the prose definition is quoted rather than the equation; the equation layout was read from the page text."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Attention_p enters (1) and (2) as one variable","source_object_ids":["S1"],"relation":"partial","note":"Three candidate measures with different scales are given for one coefficient; no definition is given for (2)."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"Choose one operational definition of Attention_p (or index the variants, for example Ceremony_p, Stories_p, Outlets_p) and state the same definition in (2); say how δ differs between binary and count measures."},{"id":"MODEL-005","category":"notation_inconsistency","finding_summary":"In eq. (2), Near_j is indexed only by listing j although it is defined relative to project p, and the area index a(j) and the comparison group are not defined.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"low","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3 Effect on housing listings","text_quote":"Nearj indicates the inner distance band","precision":"exact"},"claim_text":"Y_jpt indicates that listing j near project p in month t mentions cycling; Near_j indicates the inner distance band; α_a(j) and λ_pt are area and project-by-month fixed effects.","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"Y has indices j, p and t, so the data appear to be listing-by-project pairs, but Near_j carries no p index. A listing within the inner band of one project may lie in the outer band of another. The text says only 'the inner distance band', so the band widths and the 'further away' comparison group are not defined. The area a(j) is also not defined and may be at a different level from the distance bands. The paper presents the model as a possible example, so this is an incomplete notation issue, not a computational error.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 11 text (eq. 2 and definitions)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md","page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3","text_quote":"Nearj indicates the inner distance band","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Equation (2) read from the page text; subscripts are fragmented in extraction."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Near_j is a listing-level indicator in a listing-by-project panel","source_object_ids":["S1"],"relation":"partial","note":"Near depends on the project but is not indexed by p."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"Index the indicator as Near_jp, define the distance bands and the comparison group, and define the area a(j)."},{"id":"MODEL-006","category":"equation_text_mismatch","finding_summary":"Eq. (2) interacts Near×Post with Attention_p but includes no Near_j×Attention_p term, and the stated fixed effects do not obviously absorb it, so δ is not a standard triple-difference coefficient.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"medium","confidence":"low","location":{"page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3 Effect on housing listings","text_quote":"are area and project-by-month fixed","precision":"partial"},"claim_text":"Eq. (2) tests whether openings increase mentions nearby (θ > 0) and more so when the opening is well known locally (δ > 0).","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"The displayed regressors are Near×Post and Near×Post×Attention, with area fixed effects α_a(j) and project-by-month fixed effects λ_pt. Post and Post×Attention are absorbed by λ_pt, because both vary only by project and month. Near_j×Attention_p varies across listings within a project, so it is absorbed by neither fixed effect set unless area effects are interacted with project, and the paper does not say so. Without that term, δ also picks up any baseline difference between near and far listings that scales with Attention_p. This is a specification gap in a proposed model. The paper does not state the estimand, so low confidence.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 11 text (eq. 2)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md","page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3","text_quote":"are area and project-by-month fixed","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Fragment from the sentence defining the fixed effects; the equation itself is fragmented in extraction."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"δ identifies the extra effect of local attention","source_object_ids":["S1"],"relation":"partial","note":"The lower-order Near×Attention term is missing and not clearly absorbed."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"Include Near×Attention_p (or fixed effects that absorb it) and state which terms are absorbed by α_a(j) and λ_pt."},{"id":"MODEL-007","category":"model_interpretation_overreach","finding_summary":"The hypothesis θ > 0 is described as 'openings increase mentions of cycling nearby', but in eq. (2) θ is the Near×Post effect only at Attention_p = 0.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"low","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3 Effect on housing listings","text_quote":"openings increase mentions of cycling nearby","precision":"partial"},"claim_text":"The hypotheses are θ > 0 and δ > 0: openings increase mentions of cycling nearby, and more so when the opening is well known locally.","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"With the interaction δ Near×Post×Attention in the model, θ is the effect for Attention_p = 0, which is a project without a ceremony if Attention is binary, or zero stories or outlets if it is a count (the latter cannot occur for projects identified from articles). The average effect of openings across projects is θ + δ·E[Attention]. The prose attributes the average effect to θ alone.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 11 text (hypotheses)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md","page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3","text_quote":"openings increase mentions of cycling nearby","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Quote is part of the hypothesis sentence."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"θ > 0 means openings increase nearby mentions","source_object_ids":["S1"],"relation":"partial","note":"θ is conditional on Attention_p = 0 given the interaction term."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"Define θ as the effect at Attention_p = 0 or centre Attention_p, and state the average-effect hypothesis explicitly."},{"id":"MODEL-008","category":"model_interpretation_overreach","finding_summary":"The Section 6.3 diagnosis and remedy for misdated openings ('bias toward zero'; restrict model (2) to precisely dated projects) do not follow from the displayed setup, because misdating differs by Attention and the precisely dated sample is almost all ceremony projects.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"medium","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":12,"page_label":"12","section":"6.3 Proposed econometric analysis","text_quote":"bias the results toward zero","precision":"partial"},"claim_text":"If projects with less attention are dated less precisely, this could bias the results toward zero; if so, model (2) should only use precisely dated projects.","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"Eq. (2) estimates θ and δ. If Post is misassigned more for low-Attention projects, the attenuation differs by Attention, so δ can be biased upward and not only toward zero. Separately, the paper reports that 17 of the 19 day-dated projects have a ceremony (2 of the 26 without a ceremony are day-dated). Restricting to day-precision projects therefore leaves almost no variation in a ceremony-based Attention_p, which is the variable δ relies on. The remedy and the Attention interaction cannot both be implemented as described.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 12 text (bias discussion)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_012.md","page":12,"page_label":"12","section":"6.3","text_quote":"bias the results toward zero","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-a6c6d31e7be45f25b775aa47","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Fragment of the bias sentence."},{"id":"S2","type":"text","label":"page 11 text (ceremony and precision counts)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md","page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3","text_quote":"Of these, 17 can be dated to the day","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"The full sentence continues 'compared with 2 of the 26 projects without a reported ceremony'."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Misdating biases results toward zero and restricting to precisely dated projects fixes it","source_object_ids":["S1","S2"],"relation":"partial","note":"Differential misclassification can bias δ upward, and the precise subsample has little variation in ceremony."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"State the direction of bias separately for θ and δ, and either use an Attention measure that varies within precisely dated projects or describe a different remedy, such as a date-uncertainty window."},{"id":"MODEL-009","category":"model_interpretation_overreach","finding_summary":"The statement that date precision 'depends on how much attention an opening receives' is drawn from 17 of 18 ceremony projects versus 2 of 26 non-ceremony projects, but a reported opening ceremony is likely to state the opening date by construction.","issue_type":"manuscript_issue","severity":"low","confidence":"medium","location":{"page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3 Attention and date precision","text_quote":"Of these, 17 can be dated to the day","precision":"partial"},"claim_text":"This suggests that the precision of the dates is not random but depends on how much attention an opening receives.","assessment":"partially","cannot_verify_reason":null,"evidence_summary":"The comparison is descriptive and has no controls, as the paper acknowledges by proposing eq. (1). The ceremony indicator is built from the same articles that are used to date the project, so an article reporting a ceremony will usually state the opening day. The pattern therefore does not separate attention from a mechanical link between the Attention measure and the dating source. The paper uses 'suggests', so this is a qualification rather than a contradiction.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 11 text","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md","page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3","text_quote":"Of these, 17 can be dated to the day","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Counts quoted from the paragraph preceding eq. (1)."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Date precision depends on attention","source_object_ids":["S1"],"relation":"partial","note":"Raw 17/18 versus 2/26 contrast; ceremony articles may state the date mechanically."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"Add that ceremony reporting may mechanically include the date, and ensure the eq. (1) test uses an Attention measure independent of how the date was found."},{"id":"MODEL-010","category":"cannot_verify","finding_summary":"Eq. (1) is described as an ordered model but gives no cut-points, no distribution for u_p and no ordering convention for the 'none, year, month, day' categories.","issue_type":"cannot_verify","severity":"low","confidence":"low","location":{"page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3 Attention and date precision","text_quote":"ordered model of date precision","precision":"partial"},"claim_text":"A possible extension is to test this at project level with an ordered model of date precision.","assessment":"cannot_verify","cannot_verify_reason":"The model is described only in one sentence and the equation glyphs are fragmented in extraction, so the link function, cut-points and category coding cannot be checked against a full specification.","evidence_summary":"Eq. (1) shows a latent index Precision*_p = β Attention_p + x'_p γ + u_p. The text names four observed categories and states that a positive β implies higher precision, but does not give the mapping from the latent variable to the categories or the distribution of u_p (ordered probit or logit). Table 6 uses 'Not stated' for the lowest category, while the text says 'none', and the date-precision field in Table 5 is 'day precision'. These do not conflict but the coding is not tied together. The paper presents the specification as an example, so the missing detail is a disclosure limit.","source_objects":[{"id":"S1","type":"text","label":"page 11 text (eq. 1 description)","path":"work/bicycle-infrastructure-paper/parsed/pages/page_011.md","page":11,"page_label":"11","section":"6.3","text_quote":"ordered model of date precision","url":null,"object_id":"OBJ-95b2618a59737e0b1725c4df","correspondence":"text","verification_note":"Prose describing eq. (1); the equation itself is fragmented in extraction."}],"claim_evidence_links":[{"claim_text":"Eq. (1) defines an ordered model of date precision","source_object_ids":["S1"],"relation":"cannot_verify","note":"Link function and cut-points are not stated."}],"numeric_check":null,"suggested_fix":"State the link function (probit or logit), the cut-points, the ordering of the four categories, and match the category names with Table 6."}],"notes":["The paper has no estimated regression tables, so the dependent-variable, fixed-effect and estimator cross-check applies only to the proposed eqs. (1) and (2), which are described as examples.","Equation glyphs and subscripts in the extracted text are fragmented, so findings on eqs. (1) and (2) rely on the prose definitions and on the equation layout as read from the page text; exact variable typography was not checked at glyph level.","Table 3 and Table 4 body cells and the Table 8 counts and shares were read from the page text, not from the structured table files, which the parser-quality auditor flagged as unreliable.","Source registry object ids were copied from the page-level text spans in source_registry.json. The page 16 image used for Figure 2 had no registry id looked up, so its object_id is null with correspondence=visual."],"completion":{"version":1,"state":"complete","scope_description":"Audit of model, equation, notation and text-equation consistency for the full parsed paper: eqs. (1) and (2) in Section 6.3, their definitions and hypotheses, the agreement tables (Tables 3 and 4), the count and share tables (Tables 6, 7 and 8), the classification rule in Appendix A.3.2 and Table 9, and the associated text, footnotes and Figure 2.","checks_completed":["Read the full parsed text and the parser-quality output before auditing","Cross-checked eq. (1) and eq. (2) against their variable definitions, fixed effects, hypotheses and prose interpretation","Checked arithmetic and definitions: 2 of 34 articles, 5 of 34 relevant, Table 3 and 4 totals and 97% agreement, Table 6 precision sum (19+10+9+5=43), 17+2=19 day-dated projects, 18+26=44, Table 8 shares and totals (266 625 + 433 954 = 700 579)","Compared Table 9 classification rule with the worked example and the Figure 2 bar colours","Compared footnote 3 with Table 3 and Section 4.1","Compared the Section 3.1.2 yield statistic with Figure 1 and Section 3.1.4"],"missing_checks":[],"evidence_limitations":["Equation glyphs and subscripts are fragmented in the extracted text, so exact typography of eqs. (1) and (2) was not checked at glyph level.","The paper contains no estimated regression tables or structural model, so no estimator, reference-group or fixed-effect checks of reported results were possible.","The overlap between pilot articles and rounds 2-4 is not documented, so the 96-article and 44-project reconciliation could not be verified."]},"coverage":[{"item_id":"assigned_remit","source_object_ids":[],"claim":null,"check_performed":"Read the full parsed text and audited eqs. (1) and (2), their definitions and interpretation, the agreement tables, the count tables, the Table 9 classification rule and the related text and footnotes.","status":"linked_finding","reason":"Ten findings: Table 9 precedence rule versus example (MODEL-001), footnote 3 versus Table 3 (MODEL-002), the 2 of 34 unit mismatch (MODEL-003), Attention definition (MODEL-004), eq. (2) notation (MODEL-005), missing lower-order term (MODEL-006), θ interpretation (MODEL-007), misdating remedy (MODEL-008), ceremony-precision overreach (MODEL-009), and ordered-model detail (MODEL-010). Scope was exhaustive for equations and sampled for descriptive tables.","finding_ids":["MODEL-001","MODEL-002","MODEL-003","MODEL-004","MODEL-005","MODEL-006","MODEL-007","MODEL-008","MODEL-009","MODEL-010"],"attempted":true,"importance":"central","scope":"exhaustive"},{"item_id":"claim:agreement-tables","source_object_ids":["OBJ-4e6da301f188ae5c7e5d0a63"],"claim":"Human-human and human-LLM relevance agreement are both 97% (33 of 34 articles), as shown in Tables 3 and 4.","check_performed":"Recomputed 3+30=33 and 33/34 from the page-7 text; read the Table 3 totals (3, 31, 4, 30, 34) and compared with the prose and with the Table 4 note on the majority of three runs.","status":"checked_no_issue","reason":"Counts and the 97% figure are consistent (33/34 = 97.1%). Table 4 body cells were taken from the page text, not the structured file.","finding_ids":[],"attempted":true,"importance":"central","scope":"exhaustive"},{"item_id":"claim:table8-shares","source_object_ids":[],"claim":"Table 8 and the Appendix A.2 text report 266 625 listings (38.1%) mentioning cycling out of 700 579.","check_performed":"Checked 266 625 + 433 954 = 700 579 and the shares 38.1, 13.4, 10.8, 10.7, 9.8, 3.8 and 1.0 against the counts, and the sum of type counts against the multi-type note.","status":"checked_no_issue","reason":"Arithmetic is consistent within rounding; the type rows exceed the total, as the table note says. Sampled arithmetic only, not a full recomputation of Figure 2.","finding_ids":[],"attempted":true,"importance":"peripheral","scope":"sampled"},{"item_id":"claim:table6-counts","source_object_ids":["OBJ-e9b90e0f75ce05ff1ce150cc"],"claim":"Table 6 reports 43 projects (Day 19, Month 10, Year 9, Not stated 5) and 29 dated to the day or month.","check_performed":"Summed the precision categories (19+10+9+5=43) and 19+10=29 from the page-10 text; compared 17+2=19 day-dated projects in Section 6.3 with the Day count.","status":"checked_no_issue","reason":"Counts agree; the 44-project base in Section 6.3 adds the Stord project (see MODEL-002).","finding_ids":[],"attempted":true,"importance":"peripheral","scope":"exhaustive"}]},"ttft_ms":2560,"type":"result","duration_ms":197245,"uuid":"23d7a96b-797c-4470-8435-774eb369b8e0","ttft_stream_ms":1404,"time_to_request_ms":153,"first_content_frame_ms":1404,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-12-08T17:18:28.888Z"}
